<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · lake</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lough Key</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lough Key is a lake in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/county-roscommon">County Roscommon</a>, Ireland, near Boyle, scattered with wooded islands. On one of them stood the Rock of Lough Cé, the island fortress of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macdermot">MacDermots</a> of Moylurg, whose family gave their name to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-loch-ce">Annals of Loch Cé</a>. The lakeshore estate of the King family, Rockingham, is now a forest park.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lough-key.jpg" alt="Lough Key" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: jbraine · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lough_Key_from_Above_2010.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The lake lies in the northern part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-shannon">River Shannon</a> drainage basin, northeast of the town of Boyle. It is fed by the Boyle River, which flows from Lough Gara through Boyle into Lough Key and then east to join the Shannon just above Carrick-on-Shannon. It covers 843.7 hectares, with an average depth of 5.1 metres, and holds over thirty wooded islands, among them Castle Island, Trinity Island, Orchard Island and Stag Island.</p><p>Its Irish name, Loch Cé, is linked to a tale of Irish mythology. Cé, the druid of the god Nuada, was wounded in the Second Battle of Mag Tuired and fled south to a plain full of flowers, where he died; when his grave was dug, a lake burst out of it and flooded the plain.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area has been inhabited for thousands of years, and the earliest record of Castle Island dates from 1184, when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/annals-of-loch-ce">Annals of Loch Cé</a> report that lightning set fire to the Rock of Loch-Cé, which they describe as a kingly residence. Trinity and Church Islands carry the ruins of medieval priories. The Annals of Boyle were compiled on Trinity Island from around 1000, and from 1253 to 1590 the Annals of Lough Key continued where they ended.</p><p>The King family acquired the land around the lake in the Cromwellian Settlement of the 17th century and renamed the area from Moylurg to Rockingham. Rockingham House, designed by the English architect John Nash, was built in 1809 and remained the seat of the Stafford-King-Harman family until a fire badly damaged it in 1957; it was demolished in 1970. A folly castle built by the family in the early 19th century stands on Castle Island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Sir Cecil Stafford-King-Harman, the last Baronet of Rockingham, ensured that the land went back to the people of Ireland through the Irish Land Commission. Lough Key Forest Park, opened in 1972 south of the lake, covers 350 hectares of the former estate and contains the remains of five ring forts. The Moylurg Tower, a viewing tower built in 1973, stands on the site of Rockingham House.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Lough Key” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The MacDermots ruled from the Rock of Lough Ce, an island fortress on <b>Lough Key</b>, and patronised the abbey of Boyle and the learned families who compiled the Annals of Loch Ce.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-moylurg">Kingdom of Moylurg</a></li></ul></section>
